tical story, but a laudatory story of the ancient Olympic Games. The book was first published in 1929, in Budapest, in Hungarian, and later in German editions in 1930 and 1935."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The silver medal was earned by German " [:a {:href "/athletes/920749"} "Ernst Weiß"] " for his story " [:em {} "Boetius von Orlamünde"] ", which depicts the life of the beggar Boetius, his recalling and confirmation in the taming of his stallion Cyrus. The story was published in 1966 under the title " [:em {} "The Aristocrat"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The bronze medal was won by the Dutch couple " [:a {:href "/athletes/921114"} "Margo Scharten-Antink"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/921113"} "Carel Scharten"] " for " [:em {} "De Nar uit de Maremmen"] " (The Fool of the Maremms). It describes the life story of a painter and also describes something somewhat “sporting“."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Catalogue Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Translated Title"] [:th {} "Original Title"] [:th {} "Title in Olympic Report or Catalogue"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/920775"} "Ferenc Mező"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "History of the Olympic Games"] [:td {} "Az olympiai játékok története"] [:td {} "L’Histoire des Jeux Olympiques (Fragments)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/920749"} "Ernst Weiß"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "Boëtius von Orlamünde (The Aristocrat)"] [:td {} "B